WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.434
An agent that watches a running kernel, writes its own eBPF controls,

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:07.421
and blocks an exploit while the service keeps running.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:08.972
That is the pitch.

00:00:08.972 --> 00:00:11.314
The repository holds the bottom of this picture.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:14.807
I'm Bastion.

00:00:14.807 --> 00:00:18.392
Shirudo is the path an agent's code has to pass through before

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:19.587
it touches a kernel.

00:00:19.587 --> 00:00:21.380
Let's follow one shield through it.

00:00:23.258 --> 00:00:27.248
This one guards Copy Fail, a bug in the kernel's crypto sockets.

00:00:27.248 --> 00:00:31.238
It sits on a security hook, the one every socket creation passes.

00:00:31.658 --> 00:00:33.268
Any crypto socket is refused.

00:00:33.268 --> 00:00:37.776
The exploit is gone, and so is every honest use of the socket family.

00:00:39.408 --> 00:00:40.584
Six kinds of traffic.

00:00:40.584 --> 00:00:44.113
With no shield, the splice into a failing decrypt writes on a

00:00:44.113 --> 00:00:45.878
page the socket does not own.

00:00:46.298 --> 00:00:47.904
The repo's detector only counts.

00:00:47.904 --> 00:00:51.439
Run it first, and you learn who splices into these sockets

00:00:51.439 --> 00:00:52.724
before you block anything.

00:00:53.144 --> 00:00:56.427
The blunt shield stops the exploit at socket creation, and four

00:00:56.427 --> 00:00:57.621
honest callers with it.

00:00:58.041 --> 00:01:02.217
The precise one waits for the send and refuses only zero-copy splices.

00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:06.392
The comment says why it can't be narrower: the operation lives in

00:01:06.392 --> 00:01:08.480
a struct with no type information.

00:01:10.408 --> 00:01:12.933
The operator host holds the only signing key.

00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:16.721
It compiles the shield into a signed loader, a program that loads

00:01:16.721 --> 00:01:18.615
the shield from inside the kernel.

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:20.500
The registry just stores bytes.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.006
Shirudo does not trust it.

00:01:22.386 --> 00:01:24.879
The target holds only the public certificate.

00:01:24.879 --> 00:01:29.152
Its kernel checks the signature, and a gate refuses every other BPF

00:01:29.152 --> 00:01:30.577
load, even from root.

00:01:32.433 --> 00:01:35.577
A changed byte fails here, before the kernel sees anything.

00:01:35.977 --> 00:01:38.525
A file re-signed with someone else's key fails here.

00:01:38.925 --> 00:01:41.985
A shield built for a kernel that renamed a field fails here.

00:01:42.385 --> 00:01:45.915
And if the TPM refuses the measurement, the load is undone.

00:01:45.915 --> 00:01:48.482
Every load and unload is on the record.

00:01:50.258 --> 00:01:51.136
It is cheap.

00:01:51.136 --> 00:01:54.648
The project measured three point two microseconds of guard time on a

00:01:54.648 --> 00:01:57.282
fork and exec, before any shield of your own.

00:01:58.533 --> 00:02:01.406
The agent itself is in the screenshots, not the repo.

00:02:01.406 --> 00:02:04.566
Codex queries the fleet, and the one thing it may do

00:02:04.566 --> 00:02:05.715
is reboot a node.

00:02:06.095 --> 00:02:07.856
Approval is the signing key.

00:02:07.856 --> 00:02:09.970
Whoever holds it decides what loads.

00:02:11.608 --> 00:02:15.283
The posts promise agents that shield running systems without downtime.

00:02:15.703 --> 00:02:19.459
The repo needs a patched kernel and a TPM, Cilium cannot run

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:23.528
beside it, and the Java demo used the path Shirudo does not sign.

00:02:25.308 --> 00:02:27.458
Let the agent write all the code it likes.

00:02:27.458 --> 00:02:29.129
Make the signing key the only door.

00:02:32.858 --> 00:02:37.163
Shirudo: shields on a hook, a signature at the door, a register that remembers.

00:02:37.583 --> 00:02:39.692
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:40.295
I'm Bastion.

00:02:40.295 --> 00:02:40.597
Bye!
